Subject: [PATCH 29/31] artist: emulate fractional RGB and Color Recovery
From 85b8027303b5ec3e5d3da31fc19a26755c978d03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ostrich <570911+ostrich@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:26:35 -0400

NEXTSTEP's original HP framebuffer driver offers RGB:888/32 on Artist,
but uses the unimplemented 0xbba0 fractional aperture. This discards its
pixel uploads and leaves the desktop black.

Convert fractional RGB transfers to the existing 8-bit image planes with
the Artist-specific 2x16 dither matrices. Respect the IBO dithering bit,
subword channel masks and pixel masks; handle RGB fills, lines and fonts.
Expand fractional reads so undithered backing-store save/restore preserves
all 256 native pixel values.

Use the primary Color Recovery control bit in MISC_CTRL to select an
edge-preserving 2x16 scanout filter, followed by independent component
lookup tables. Sliding histograms bound scanout work per pixel. Dirty the
following row when its filter inputs change, and redraw after palette or
control changes. Clear recovery selection on reset. All device-visible
state and image-plane storage already exist in VMState.

Implement the NGLE source-coordinate blit trigger used by native window
moves, retaining overlap-safe image-plane copies.

Keep the existing indexed and grayscale paths. Add fractional, drawing,
readback, recovery, palette, dirty-neighbor, reset and migration qtests,
and register the Artist tests independently of the SCSI test selection.

The filter follows HP's published functional description. Exact DAC
rounding, boundary padding and hardware-specific filter adjustments have
not been established with a physical Artist device. This does not add
multiple colormap/attribute-plane selection or claim complete Artist
hardware fidelity.

References:
https://h10032.www1.hp.com/ctg/Manual/lpv38781.pdf
https://hparchive.com/Journals/HPJ-1995-04.pdf
